 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "Rust is hydrated iron(III) oxide, written Fe₂O₃·xH₂O (x varies).\niron + oxygen + water → hydrated iron(III) oxide (rust)\nRust is soft and flaky. It falls off and exposes fresh iron underneath, so rusting goes on until all the iron is eaten away. (On aluminium, the oxide layer sticks and protects the metal.)\nRusting is an oxidation: iron gains oxygen. It is slow.\nEveryday examples: roofing sheets, bicycle chains, iron gates, nails, cars, tools left in the rain, cooking pots, railway lines, bridges and ships.",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {
   "x": "rust keeps eating into an iron gate"
  },
  "problem": "Why {x} once it starts?",
  "steps": [
   "Rust is flaky and falls off.",
   "Fresh iron underneath meets air and water and rusts too."
  ],
  "answer": "Rust does not protect the iron, so rusting continues.",
  "checks": []
 },
